  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday Night through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 1148 AM CDT Wed Jun 24 2026\n\nAlthough no potent fronts are forecast to cross the area during the \nperiod, weak disturbances aloft will present low to medium \nchances for daily showers and thunderstorms. Highest chances each \nday will be along and north of I-40, peaking Saturday at 50-70%. \nSaturday and Sunday look interesting in the guidance, as heat will\nbring abundant instability into the region, with steep lapse \nrates. Being so displaced from the forcing, like we will be this \nweekend, is a disadvantage when it comes to severe weather. The \nsevere threat, if one develops, looks more marginal/isolated with \ndamaging winds and heavy rain the main threats at this time. \nSomething to certainly keep an eye on as we approach the weekend. \n\nThe more zonal upper level pattern means the heat will be cranked\nup into the remainder of the forecast period as well. 850mb winds\nwill turn more southwesterly, advecting in heat to the mid-state.\nHighs through Saturday will gradually creep into the upper 80s \nand low 90s. A high pressure system builds right over the \nsoutheastern United States Sunday into Monday, combined with an \ninflux of Gulf moisture, warmer temperatures, and fairly stagnant \nflow will make it feel very hot with widespread heat indicies in \nthe triple digits Sunday - Wednesday. Heat risk looks to be in the\nmajor category to possibly extreme towards the end of the period,\nmeaning heat illness will occur in a shorter amount of time \noutdoors than usual. Make sure to keep this in mind if you have \nany outdoor activities in the coming days.",
